{"tool_name":"splign","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"splign","home":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sutils/splign/splign.cgi","license":"Freeware","summary":"Splign is a utility for computing cDNA-to-Genomic, or spliced sequence alignments. At the heart of the program is a compartmentization algorithm which identifies possible gene duplications, and a refined alignment algorithm recognizing introns and splice signals.","addition_date":"2021-05-27T09:15:54Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T20:20:51.667632Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Web application"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:splign","credit":[{"name":"Yuri Kapustin","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sutils/splign/splign.cgi?textpage=documentation"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Scaffolding","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3216"},{"term":"Sequence alignment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0292"},{"term":"Sequence alignment refinement","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2089"},{"term":"Sequence merging","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0232"},{"term":"Sequence trimming","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3192"}]}],"name":"Splign","operating_system":["Linux"],"owner":"Kigaard","publication":[{"doi":"10.1186/1745-6150-3-20","metadata":{"abstract":"Background: The computation of accurate alignments of cDNA sequences against a genome is at the foundation of modern genome annotation pipelines. Several factors such as presence of paralogs, small exons, non-consensus splice signals, sequencing errors and polymorphic sites pose recognized difficulties to existing spliced alignment algorithms. Results: We describe a set of algorithms behind a tool called Splign for computing cDNA-to-Genome alignments. The algorithms include a high-performance preliminary alignment, a compartment identification based on a formally defined model of adjacent duplicated regions, and a refined sequence alignment. In a series of tests, Splign has produced more accurate results than other tools commonly used to compute spliced alignments, in a reasonable amount of time. Conclusion: Splign's ability to deal with various issues complicating the spliced alignment problem makes it a helpful tool in eukaryotic genome annotation processes and alternative splicing studies. Its performance is enough to align the largest currently available pools of cDNA data such as the human EST set on a moderate-sized computing cluster in a matter of hours. The duplications identification (compartmentization) algorithm can be used independently in other areas such as the study of pseudogenes. \u00a9 2008 Kapustin et al; licensee BioMed Central Ltd.","authors":[{"name":"Kapustin Y."},{"name":"Lipman D."},{"name":"Souvorov A."},{"name":"Tatusova T."}],"citationCount":282,"date":"2008-05-21T00:00:00Z","journal":"Biology Direct","title":"Splign: Algorithms for computing spliced alignments with identification of paralogs"},"pmcid":"PMC2440734","pmid":"18495041","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Gene transcripts","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3512"},{"term":"Genetic variation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0199"},{"term":"RNA splicing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3320"},{"term":"Sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0080"},{"term":"Whole genome sequencing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3673"}]},"bioschemas":{"name":"Splign","home":"https://bio.tools/splign","license":"Freeware","summary":"Splign is a utility for computing cDNA-to-Genomic, or spliced sequence alignments. At the heart of the program is a compartmentization algorithm which identifies possible gene duplications, and a refined alignment algorithm recognizing introns and splice signals.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotool